+# The Daily Grind
+
+Static homepage served with `pnpm start` (statikk).
+
+## My Account: passkeys
+
+Click **My Account** in the nav (or go to `/#account`) to open the account drawer. Passkeys are the only way to sign in. There are no passwords.
+
+| Feature | How it works |
+| --- | --- |
+| **Create account** | Name + email → `navigator.credentials.create()` with a discoverable credential (`residentKey: 'required'`), ES256/RS256, `attestation: 'none'`. The account only exists once the passkey is verified. |
+| **Sign in** | "Sign in with a passkey" (discoverable, no username needed), email + Continue (scoped `allowCredentials`), or passkey **autofill** on the email field (`autocomplete="username webauthn"` with conditional mediation). |
+| **Manage passkeys** | Shows each passkey's provider name (from its AAGUID), when it was created and last used, sync status (BE/BS flags), and which one this session used. You can add, rename, or remove passkeys. You can't remove your last passkey, so you can't lock yourself out. |
+| **Profile** | You can change your name at any time. Changing your email (your sign-in identifier) needs you to verify again. |
+| **Reauthentication** | Adding or removing a passkey, changing your email, or deleting your account needs a passkey check (`userVerification: 'required'`) within the last 5 minutes. A fresh sign-in counts. When the API returns `reauth_required`, the UI shows a "Confirm it's you" dialog and then retries. |
+| **Signal API** | When supported, `signalUnknownCredential`, `signalAllAcceptedCredentials` and `signalCurrentUserDetails` keep passkey managers in sync after sign-in, removals, profile edits and account deletion. |
+
+### Files
+
+- `js/account.js`: the UI controller (ceremonies, rendering, reauth and confirm dialogs).
+- `js/passkey-server.js`: the relying party / account API.
+- `js/webauthn.js`: base64url, CBOR, authenticator-data parsing, COSE→JWK conversion, signature verification, and JSON (de)serialisation.
+- `css/account.css`: drawer and dialog styles.
+
+### Important: the relying party currently runs in the browser
+
+This site has no backend yet. So `passkey-server.js` acts as the server: it runs in the page and saves its data to `localStorage`. It still performs every WebAuthn verification step for real:
+
+- challenges are single-use and expire
+- `clientDataJSON` type and origin are checked
+- the RP ID hash is checked
+- the UP and UV flags are checked
+- signatures are verified against the stored public key with WebCrypto
+- signature counters are checked
+
+The UI only talks to it through JSON requests that mirror HTTP endpoints (`/api/passkeys/login/options`, …).
+
+**Before production**, port `passkey-server.js` to a real backend:
+
+1. Store users, credentials and challenges in a database.
+2. Replace the session token with an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie.
+3. Swap the `api()` function in `account.js` for `fetch()`.
+
+Data saved in the browser can be read and changed by the user, so the in-browser version gives no real security guarantees.
+
+Passkeys need a secure context: use `http://localhost:<port>` or HTTPS on a real domain. IP addresses such as `127.0.0.1` are rejected by browsers as an RP ID.
